"""Class 10 SST Ch 3 The Making of a Global World - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"Corn Laws were abolished in Britain after pressure from industrialists and urban dwellers.", r"Corn Laws restricted the import of corn, which kept food prices high.", "a",
     r"Unhappy with high food prices, industrialists and townspeople forced the abolition; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"The Bretton Woods conference was held in July 1944 in New Hampshire, USA.", r"The IMF and the World Bank are also called the Bretton Woods twins.", "b",
     r"Both are true, but the second statement does not explain the venue or date of the conference."),
 AR4(r"Indian indentured labourers were taken to Caribbean islands, Mauritius and Fiji.", r"Indentured labourers enjoyed high wages and good living conditions on plantations.", "c",
     r"A is true. They faced harsh conditions and few legal rights, so R is false."),
 AR4(r"After the Industrial Revolution in Britain, Indian cotton textile exports to Europe increased.", r"British manufacturers pressured the government to impose duties on cotton imports.", "d",
     r"Indian cotton exports declined because of duties and competition from machine-made Manchester cloth, so A is false; R is true."),
 AR4(r"Rinderpest destroyed the livelihoods of many Africans in the 1890s.", r"Rinderpest killed about 90 per cent of the cattle in Africa.", "a",
     r"Cattle loss meant loss of food, income and transport; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Wheat prices in India fell by about 50 per cent between 1928 and 1934.", r"International prices crashed during the Great Depression.", "a",
     r"Indian prices followed the international crash; R explains A."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In the nineteenth century, hundreds of thousands of Indians and Chinese went to work on plantations, in mines and in road and railway construction around the world. In India, indentured labourers were hired under contracts which promised return travel to India after five years on the employer's plantation. Most came from eastern Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, central India and dry districts of Tamil Nadu. "
   r"(i) What is an indentured labourer? (ii) After how many years did the contract promise return travel? (iii) Name two destinations of Indian indentured labour. (iv) Why did poor people agree to migrate despite the risks?",
   r"(i) A bonded labourer under contract to work for an employer for a fixed period to repay the cost of passage (1). (ii) Five years (1). (iii) Any two of Trinidad, Guyana, Surinam, Mauritius, Fiji (1). (iv) Poverty, debts, unemployment and the decline of cottage industries; agents also gave false promises of work and a better life (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> The Great Depression began around 1929 and lasted until the mid-1930s. Most parts of the world saw catastrophic falls in production, employment, incomes and trade. Agricultural regions and communities were the worst affected because the fall in agricultural prices was greater and longer lasting than the fall in industrial prices. The US was among the hardest hit; many banks collapsed. "
   r"(i) When did the Great Depression begin? (ii) Which sector was worst affected? (iii) Name one economy badly hit by it. (iv) Why did the crisis in the US spread to other parts of the world?",
   r"(i) Around 1929 (1). (ii) Agriculture (1). (iii) The USA (any correct economy, e.g. India or Europe) (1). (iv) American banks stopped lending and recalled loans from Europe, and the US raised import duties; this reduced world trade and hit other countries dependent on US loans and exports (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In July 1944 representatives of 44 countries met at Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, USA. The conference set up the International Monetary Fund (IMF) to deal with external surpluses and deficits of its member nations and the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (World Bank) to finance post-war reconstruction. The system worked on fixed exchange rates. "
   r"(i) Where and when was the conference held? (ii) What is the function of the IMF? (iii) Give the full form of the World Bank's original name. (iv) Why did the Allies want such a framework after the Second World War?",
   r"(i) Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, USA; July 1944 (1). (ii) To deal with external surpluses and deficits of member nations (1). (iii) International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (1). (iv) To preserve economic stability and full employment in the industrial world and avoid a repeat of the instability of the interwar years and the Great Depression (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

